wedge(SimplicialComplex,SimplicialComplex,RingElement,RingElement) -- make the wedge sum of two abstract simplicial complexes
*****************************************************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note wedge: wedge_lpSimplicialComplex_cmSimplicialComplex_
    cmRingElement_cmRingElement_rp,
  * Usage: 
        wedge (Delta, Gamma, u, v)
  * Inputs:
      * Delta, an *note abstract simplicial complex: SimplicialComplex,, 
      * Gamma, an *note abstract simplicial complex: SimplicialComplex,, 
      * u, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, a vertex of
        $\Delta$
      * v, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, a vertex of
        $\Gamma$
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Variables => a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value {}, that
        provides variables for the polynomial ring in which the wedge sum is
        represented by its Stanley–Reisner ideal
  * Outputs:
      * an *note abstract simplicial complex: SimplicialComplex,, the wedge sum
        of $\Delta$ and $\Gamma$ obtained by identifying the vertices $u$ and
        $v$

Description
===========

For any two abstract simplicial complexes $\Delta$ and $\Gamma$ with
distinguished vertices $u$ and $v$, the wedge sum is the simplicial complex
formed by taking the disjoint union of $\Delta$ and $\Gamma$ and then
identifying $u$ and $v$.

The bowtie complex is the wedge sum of two 2-simplicies

+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = QQ[a,b,c];                                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : Δ = simplexComplex(2, S)                                      |
|                                                                   |
|o2 = simplicialComplex | abc |                                     |
|                                                                   |
|o2 : SimplicialComplex                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : R = QQ[d,e,f];                                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : Γ = simplexComplex(2, R)                                      |
|                                                                   |
|o4 = simplicialComplex | def |                                     |
|                                                                   |
|o4 : SimplicialComplex                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : ΔvΓ = wedge (Δ, Γ, a, f)                                      |
|                                                                   |
|o5 = simplicialComplex | ade abc |                                 |
|                                                                   |
|o5 : SimplicialComplex                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : vertices ΔvΓ                                                  |
|                                                                   |
|o6 = {a, b, c, d, e}                                               |
|                                                                   |
|o6 : List                                                          |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : assert (# gens ring ΔvΓ === # gens ring Δ + # gens ring Γ - 1)|
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

When the optional argument $\mathrm{Variables}$ is used, the wedge sum is
represented by its Stanley–Reisner ideal in a new polynomial ring having this
list as variables.  The variables in the ring of $\Delta$ corresponds to the
first few variables in this new polynomial ring and the variables in the ring
of $\Gamma$ correspond to the next few variables in $R$ (omitting the variable
corresponding to $v$).  This option is particularly convenient when taking the
wedge sum of two abstract simplical complexes already defined in the same ring.

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : ΔvΓ' = wedge (Δ, Γ, a, d, Variables => toList(x_0..x_4))|
|                                                             |
|o8 = simplicialComplex | x_0x_3x_4 x_0x_1x_2 |               |
|                                                             |
|o8 : SimplicialComplex                                       |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : vertices ΔvΓ'                                           |
|                                                             |
|o9 = {x , x , x , x , x }                                    |
|       0   1   2   3   4                                     |
|                                                             |
|o9 : List                                                    |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : ΔvΓ'' = wedge (Δ, Δ, a, a, Variables => {a,b,c,d,e})   |
|                                                             |
|o10 = simplicialComplex | ade abc |                          |
|                                                             |
|o10 : SimplicialComplex                                      |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : vertices ΔvΓ''                                         |
|                                                             |
|o11 = {a, b, c, d, e}                                        |
|                                                             |
|o11 : List                                                   |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : ring ΔvΓ''                                             |
|                                                             |
|o12 = QQ[a..e]                                               |
|                                                             |
|o12 : PolynomialRing                                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

When the variables in the ring of $\Delta$ and the ring of $\Gamma$ are not
disjoint, names of vertices in the wedge sum may not be intelligible; the same
name will apparently be used for two distinct variables.

See also
========

  * *note Making an abstract simplicial complex: Making an abstract simplicial
    complex, -- information about the basic constructors
  * *note vertices(SimplicialComplex): vertices_lpSimplicialComplex_rp, -- get
    the list of the vertices for an abstract simplicial complex
  * *note SimplicialComplex * SimplicialComplex: SimplicialComplex _st
    SimplicialComplex, -- make the join for two abstract simplicial complexes
  * *note substitute(SimplicialComplex,PolynomialRing):
    substitute_lpSimplicialComplex_cmPolynomialRing_rp, -- change the ring of a
    simplicial complex
  * *note elementaryCollapse:
    elementaryCollapse_lpSimplicialComplex_cmRingElement_rp, -- construct the
    elementary collapse of a free face in a simplicial complex

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note wedge(SimplicialComplex,SimplicialComplex,RingElement,RingElement):
    wedge_lpSimplicialComplex_cmSimplicialComplex_cmRingElement_cmRingElement_
    rp, -- make the wedge sum of two abstract simplicial complexes
