Working with simplicial maps -- information about simplicial maps and the induced operations
********************************************************************************************

Let $C$ and $D$ be simplicial complexes. A simplicial map is a map $f : C \to
D$ such that for any face $F \subset C$, we have that $f(F)$ is contained in a
face of $D$.

Although the primary method for creating a simplicial map is *note
map(SimplicialComplex,SimplicialComplex,Matrix):
map_lpSimplicialComplex_cmSimplicialComplex_cmMatrix_rp,, there are a few other
constructors.

Making simplicial maps
======================

  * *note map(SimplicialComplex,SimplicialComplex,Matrix):
    map_lpSimplicialComplex_cmSimplicialComplex_cmMatrix_rp, -- create a
    simplicial map between simplicial complexes
  * *note id _ SimplicialComplex: id _us SimplicialComplex, -- make the
    identity map from a SimplicialComplex to itself
  * *note SimplicialMap: SimplicialMap, -- the class of all maps between
    simplicial complexes
  * *note isWellDefined(SimplicialMap): isWellDefined_lpSimplicialMap_rp, --
    whether underlying data is uncontradictory

Having made a *note simplicial map: SimplicialMap,, one can access its basic
invariants or test for some elementary properties by using the following
methods. Having made a *note map of abstract simplicial complexes:
SimplicialMap,, one can access its basic invariants or test for some elementary
properties by using the following methods.

Determining attributes and properties of simplicial maps
========================================================

  * *note source(SimplicialMap): source_lpSimplicialMap_rp, -- get the source
    of the map
  * *note target(SimplicialMap): target_lpSimplicialMap_rp, -- get the target
    of the map
  * *note matrix(SimplicialMap): matrix_lpSimplicialMap_rp, -- get the
    underlying map of rings

